ralph/progress.txt:209:- Repository CRUD-and-invariants tests for a **tenant-scoped service leaf table whose public surface is Insert + GetByID + ListByService + Update + DeleteByID, whose row has database-owned version/created_at/updated_at, and whose parent link is the composite `(organization_id, service_id)` FK** (BE-0475 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/service_domain_repository_invariants_test.go for service_domains). The reusable shape is: seed the Organization -> Project -> Environment -> Service chain with existing helpers, insert through the typed repository rather than raw SQL, assert version=1 and created_at==updated_at on INSERT, assert Update bumps version and advances updated_at while preserving created_at and identity fields, assert matching If-Match succeeds while stale If-Match on Update/Delete returns typed `E_CONFLICT`, assert DeleteByID returns the final row and removes it, and cover CHECK/FK/UNIQUE violations, transaction rollback, parent cascade, deterministic ListByService ordering, and nil-Tx guards. Forward-applicable to future service-scoped leaf tables with hard-delete lifecycle and optimistic concurrency.
ralph/progress.txt:210:- Repository CRUD-and-invariants tests for a **TABLE WHOSE *_repository.go + PRIMARY *_test.go + INTERNAL *_internal_test.go + CANONICAL *_canonical_test.go ALL ALREADY EXIST FROM A PRIOR (NON-PRD) SUBSYSTEM STORY + VALIDATOR-EXHAUSTIVENESS STORY, AND WHOSE LIFECYCLE IS APPEND + READ + ONE STATE-TRANSITION (MarkRevoked, *Tx-bound) WITH A REPOSITORY-LEVEL LIMIT CAP, AND WHOSE UPDATE SURFACE IS GUARDED BY THREE STACKED BEFORE-UPDATE TRIGGERS (set_updated_at + bump_version + a per-column rewrite-rejecting trigger), AND WHOSE Append FILLS A ZERO StartedAt SERVER-SIDE IN THE REPOSITORY (not via DB DEFAULT)** (BE-0473 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/break_glass_repository_invariants_test.go for break_glass_sessions). Subspecies of the BE-0469 (idempotency_keys) gap-fill pattern with five load-bearing deltas: (a) the THREE-trigger UPDATE surface is unique in store — the rewrite-rejecting trigger (break_glass_sessions_reject_rewrite) raises SQLSTATE 23001 (restrict_violation) for any UPDATE that mutates a non-revocation column AND for any UPDATE that blanks the reason column. The test file walks the FULL trigger predicate matrix (id, organization_id, actor_id, actor_kind, reason, started_at, expires_at, request_id, correlation_id) via raw-SQL UPDATEs and asserts BOTH the typed error AND byte-identical row re-load AFTER the rejection. A regression that demoted any single column from the trigger predicate would surface as a per-case failure on its first iteration. The existing primary _test.go's TestBreakGlassSchemaRejectsUpdateToImmutableColumns covers ONLY reason rewrite, so the per-column-matrix invariants probe is genuinely new coverage. (b) The Append fills a zero StartedAt server-side via `if s.StartedAt.IsZero() { s.StartedAt = time.Now().UTC() }` BEFORE the INSERT — the column has NO DEFAULT in the migration (vs created_at/updated_at which DO have DEFAULT now()). A regression that removed the IsZero branch would NOT surface as a CHECK violation (expires_at > started_at is satisfied with zero started_at + any future expires_at) — it would silently store year-0001 rows. TestBreakGlassRepositoryAppendStartedAtDefaultsToNow is the regression backstop: it asserts the filled StartedAt falls within a ±2s wallclock bracket. (c) MarkRevoked's UPDATE filters on `revoked_at IS NULL` (column predicate, NOT row-existence predicate) — same shape as DriftFindingRepository.MarkResolved (BE-0471). The zero-rows branch falls through to a tenant-scoped follow-up Get inside the SAME *Tx to distinguish "missing" (typed apierr.NotFound, caller-supplied id) from "already revoked" (typed apierr.Conflict). This means TestBreakGlassRepositoryMarkRevokedCrossTenantIsNotFound is NOT redundant with TestBreakGlassRepositoryGetCrossTenantIsNotFound: a regression that dropped tenant scoping from BOTH the UPDATE and the follow-up Get would silently succeed in revoking another tenant's session. The cross-tenant Get + cross-tenant MarkRevoked pair pins both halves of the contract; either alone is insufficient. (d) The CHECK / FK violation matrix for break_glass_sessions has FOUR distinct probes that surface as typed apierr.Conflict via mapWriteError: unknown organization (FK), blank actor_kind + unknown actor_kind (both fail the CHECK actor_kind IN ('usr','sa')), blank reason (CHECK length(reason) > 0), and expires_at <= started_at (CHECK expires_at > started_at — exercised with both equal-to-started_at AND predates-started_at sub-cases). The repository does NOT pre-validate these fields (the service buildSessionToCreate is the chokepoint for validation), so the constraint violation flows through the database CHECK and surfaces via mapWriteError. (e) The `twoOrgs(t, db, f, label)` helper is genuinely new at package scope (verified via grep on `^func twoOrgs` across all store_test files — no prior declaration exists) — it names the two-organization fixture pattern (a TARGET tenant + a SUPPORT actor's home tenant) that every break_glass test needs. The 28-test shape this file lands: Append-row-shape (raw-SQL byte-equal + bgs_ prefix + server-side timestamps within ±2s + version=1 + RevokedAt nil), Append-respects-caller-supplied-id, Append-duplicate-id-Conflict (PRIMARY KEY), Append-StartedAt-defaults-to-now (the load-bearing server-side-fill probe), four CHECK/FK-violation Conflicts (unknown-org, blank-actor_kind, unknown-actor_kind, blank-reason), expires_at-not-after-started_at-Conflict (equal + predate sub-cases), Append-nil-Tx-Internal, Append-transaction-rollback, Append-peer-row-byte-identical, Get-reads-back, Get-unknown-NotFound, Get-cross-tenant-NotFound, Get-expired-returns-row-as-is (Active(t) observation, NOT Get filtering), List-newest-first-ordering (started_at DESC, id DESC), List-limit-clamping matrix (non-positive / negative / above-cap / in-range), List-unknown-org-empty-slice, MarkRevoked-happy-path (RETURNING byte-equal raw-SQL + version+1 via bump_version + updated_at advance via set_updated_at), MarkRevoked-already-revoked-Conflict (does not move revoked_at/revoked_by_*), MarkRevoked-unknown-NotFound, MarkRevoked-cross-tenant-NotFound (does not touch row), MarkRevoked-nil-Tx-Internal, MarkRevoked-peer-row-byte-identical, append-mostly-trigger-rejects-column-rewrites (per-column matrix), append-mostly-trigger-rejects-blank-reason (trigger's leading guard), organization-DELETE-cascade. New helpers introduced (per-file BreakGlass-suffix to avoid store_test collisions): rawBreakGlassRow, loadBreakGlassRowByID, countBreakGlassRowsForOrg, mintBreakGlassSessionID, breakGlassSessionFixture, seedBreakGlassSession, assertBreakGlassRowByteIdentical, breakGlassTxRollbackSentinel, sanitizeTestNameForBreakGlassID, AND the package-new twoOrgs helper for the two-org fixture shape. REUSED verbatim from sibling files: assertTimePtrEqual (drift_finding_repository_invariants_test.go), seedOrg (schema_test.go), newStore (store_test.go), wantErrCode (idempotency_test.go). NO migration added (0019_break_glass_sessions landed in an early migration). Forward-applicable to ANY future repository CRUD-and-invariants story whose table carries a three-trigger UPDATE surface (set_updated_at + bump_version + rewrite-rejecting) AND whose Append fills a zero timestamp server-side in the repository rather than via DB DEFAULT — the per-column trigger-predicate matrix and the ±2s wallclock-bracket pattern are the chokepoints that catch the two most common regression shapes (a demoted trigger column or a removed IsZero default-fill branch).
ralph/progress.txt:212:- Repository CRUD-and-invariants tests for a **TABLE WHOSE *_repository.go + PRIMARY *_test.go + INTERNAL *_internal_test.go + READER ADAPTER + READER ADAPTER TEST ALL ALREADY EXIST FROM A PRIOR (NON-PRD) SUBSYSTEM STORY, AND WHOSE LIFECYCLE IS APPEND-ONLY WITH ONLY ONE WRITE SURFACE (Append) AND ONE READ SURFACE (ListByOrganization, no GetByID) AND A REPOSITORY-LEVEL LIMIT CAP, AND WHOSE INSERT COLUMN LIST DELIBERATELY OMITS occurred_at + created_at (so DEFAULT now() applies and caller-supplied timestamps are SILENTLY DISCARDED)** (BE-0467 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/audit_repository_invariants_test.go for audit_events). Subspecies of the BE-0461 (provisioning_jobs) gap-fill pattern with five load-bearing deltas: (a) the typed repository is the NARROWEST in the store package — Append + ListByOrganization only, NO GetByID, NO Update, NO Delete (row removal reachable only through organizations ON DELETE CASCADE on tenant teardown). The acceptance-criteria phrase "create typed repository methods for creating, reading, updating, and deleting ... where the lifecycle requires it" narrows to "Append + List only; the audit log lifecycle structurally rejects per-row Update / Delete" and the file header MUST document this as load-bearing so a future reader does not mistake the absence for a coverage gap; (b) the audit_events INSERT column list (auditEventColumns minus occurred_at/created_at) deliberately omits both timestamp columns, so DEFAULT now() applies regardless of caller. A test `TestAuditRepositoryAppendIgnoresCallerSuppliedTimestamps` is the regression backstop — a future refactor that added occurred_at to the INSERT (so a caller could back-date an audit record) would silently break the "audit timestamps are server-side" guarantee unless this test was also updated. The probe seeds OccurredAt=year-2000 and asserts the persisted row's OccurredAt is within [now-1s, now+1s], NOT the back-dated value; (c) CHECK constraints that the application does NOT pre-validate (blank action / blank resource_kind / blank reason / actor_kind outside the closed-set ('', 'usr', 'sa', 'system')) MUST be exercised: Append does NOT pre-check these fields (the audit service is the chokepoint for the principal-mapping inputs), so the constraint violation flows through the database CHECK and surfaces as typed `apierr.Conflict` via mapWriteError. Each test asserts BOTH the error code AND `countAuditRowsForOrg(org.ID) == 0` to pin "the rejection left no row behind"; (d) the repository-level limit cap (`auditEventListMaxLimit = 200`) is a public invariant of the ListByOrganization contract and MUST be exercised in three branches — non-positive (limit <= 0 -> 200), above-cap (limit > 200 -> 200), in-range (limit in (0, 200] honoured verbatim). A regression that returned the empty slice when limit <= 0 (or every row when limit was unbounded) would surface here. The seeding helper `seedAuditRowsRawSQL` bypasses the repository and uses direct INSERT with caller-controlled occurred_at so 201 rows can be planted in O(n) without sleeping; (e) the secondary-sort ORDER BY id DESC tiebreaker on tied occurred_at is the load-bearing list-ordering invariant: a UI rendering a pair of audit events written in the same Postgres microsecond would flicker without it. The probe seeds TWO rows with the SAME occurred_at (via raw INSERT — Append's DEFAULT now() would advance between calls) and asserts listed[0].ID > listed[1].ID lexicographically. The 16-test shape this file lands: Append-row-shape (raw-SQL byte-equal + aud_ prefix + server-side timestamps within [before, after]), Append-respects-caller-supplied-id, Append-ignores-caller-supplied-timestamps (the load-bearing server-side-timestamp probe), four CHECK-violation Conflicts (blank-action, blank-resource_kind, blank-reason, unknown-actor_kind), duplicate-id-Conflict (PRIMARY KEY), Append-transaction-rollback (sentinel error + countAuditRowsForOrg == 0), Append-cascades-from-organization-delete (orgA wipe + orgB bystander byte-identity across id/organization_id/action/reason/occurred_at/created_at), Append-peer-row-byte-identical (the BE-0468 byte-identity bystander predecessor — peer Append must NOT touch a prior row), List-orders-id-DESC-on-timestamp-tie, three List-limit-clamping branches (non-positive, above-cap, in-range), List-unknown-org-returns-empty. New helpers (per-file Audit-suffix to avoid store_test collisions): rawAuditRow, loadAuditRowByID, countAuditRowsForOrg (the per-org counter — organization is the only parent FK for audit_events, so this is also the natural per-parent counter), mintAuditID, auditTxRollbackSentinel, seedAuditRowsRawSQL (the raw-INSERT batch seeder for cap-boundary tests). Reused verbatim from sibling audit_test.go: auditEventFixture, appendAudit, assertAuditEventByteIdentical; from schema_test.go: seedOrg; from store_test.go: newStore. NO migration added. Forward-applicable to any future first-typed-repository story whose underlying table is append-only with a single (Append, List) surface and a repository-level limit cap (a future security_events / system_audit table would inherit the same template, including the server-side-timestamp invariant if the INSERT column list omits the timestamp legs).
ralph/progress.txt:213:- Repository CRUD-and-invariants tests for a **TABLE WHOSE *_repository.go + PRIMARY *_test.go ALREADY EXIST FROM A PRIOR (NON-PRD) SUBSYSTEM STORY, AND WHOSE LIFECYCLE HAS A CLAIM PROTOCOL (Claim + Complete + Release + Find — three writes, one read) DRIVEN BY AN UPSERT WITH AN ON-CONFLICT TARGET ON A NON-PK UNIQUE TUPLE (organization_id, principal_id, idempotency_key), AND WHOSE ROW BODY CARRIES A MULTI-COLUMN COMPLETION-CONSISTENT CHECK** (BE-0469 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/idempotency_repository_invariants_test.go for idempotency_keys). Subspecies of the BE-0467 (audit_events) gap-fill pattern with five load-bearing deltas: (a) the Claim INSERT uses `ON CONFLICT (organization_id, principal_id, idempotency_key) DO UPDATE` rather than the canonical `ON CONFLICT (id)` -- the PK is the secondary disambiguator, not the conflict target. So a duplicate id on a DIFFERENT (organization_id, principal_id, idempotency_key) tuple is NOT caught by ON CONFLICT; the PK violation flows through the database and surfaces through mapWriteError as apierr.Conflict. The TestIdempotencyRepositoryClaimDuplicateIDIsConflict probe MUST force the collision through caller-supplied ids on different (principal, key) tuples; a regression that switched the ON CONFLICT target to (id) would swallow the collision and silently rewrite a peer row. (b) The `idempotency_keys_completion_consistent` CHECK requires (response_status, response_body, completed_at) to be either ALL NULL (pending) or ALL NOT NULL (completed). The Complete method coerces a nil `body []byte` argument to `[]byte{}` (zero-length but non-nil) BEFORE the UPDATE so the CHECK's `response_body IS NOT NULL` clause holds for empty envelopes. TestIdempotencyRepositoryCompleteCoercesNilBodyToEmpty pins both the application-layer coercion AND the database-side non-NULL empty-bytea storage. The pending-side of the CHECK is pinned by TestIdempotencyRepositoryClaimMintsRowShape (a fresh Claim must leave all three completion columns NULL); the completed-side by TestIdempotencyRepositoryCompleteRowShape (after Complete, all three must be non-NULL AND a raw-SQL re-load must byte-equal the returned struct, AND created_at + expires_at must NOT have drifted across the Complete UPDATE -- only the four completion columns transition). (c) The Complete method's `now time.Time` argument is the only application-controlled timestamp on the table: created_at + expires_at are caller-supplied via Claim and completed_at can be caller-supplied via Complete's now arg (zero defaults to time.Now().UTC()). TestIdempotencyRepositoryCompleteHonoursExplicitNow uses a specific 2024 timestamp far from time.Now() so a regression that ignored the arg and wrote time.Now() would fail with an obvious delta. This is a structurally distinct invariant from BE-0467 (audit_events) where ALL timestamps are server-side via DEFAULT now() and a caller-supplied OccurredAt must be discarded. (d) The id minter newIdempotencyID emits `idk_<32hex>` (16 bytes of crypto/rand entropy hex-encoded — 4 + 32 = 36 chars total). The row-shape probe MUST pin BOTH the prefix AND the total length so a regression that shortened the entropy or reformatted the prefix surfaces here. Test-local mintIdempotencyID(t, suffix) follows the established per-file suffixed-helper pattern (`idk_`+t.Name()+`_`+suffix) so parallel tests that need a deterministic caller-supplied id (to force a PK collision) cannot collide. (e) The Release method has NO pre-validation coverage in the primary idempotency_test.go's TestIdempotencyClaimValidation (which only covers Claim nil-tx + Claim missing-fields + Complete nil-tx + Complete out-of-range-status). The invariants file fills the Release gap with TestIdempotencyRepositoryReleaseRejectsNilTx so all three mutating methods (Claim / Complete / Release) have a pinned nil-tx guard. The 15-test shape this file lands: Claim-row-shape (raw-SQL byte-equal + idk_ prefix + 36-char length + server-side created_at + ExpiresAt round-trip + pending-side completion-consistent), Claim-respects-caller-supplied-id, Claim-duplicate-id-Conflict (PK collision on different (org, principal, key)), Claim-unknown-organization-Conflict (FK violation), Complete-row-shape (completed-side completion-consistent + created_at/expires_at non-drift), Complete-honours-explicit-now (specific 2024 timestamp), Complete-coerces-nil-body-to-empty (non-NULL empty bytea), Release-rejects-nil-tx, Release-on-completed-is-noop (row stays in place), Release-on-missing-key-is-noop, Claim-transaction-rollback (sentinel + countIdempotencyRowsForOrg == 0), Cascade-from-organization-delete (orgA wipe + orgB bystander byte-identity across id/route/request_hash/request_id/status/created_at/expires_at), Claim-peer-row-byte-identical, Find-returns-expired-row-as-is (Find does NOT filter on expires_at > now()), ExpiresAt-round-trip (microsecond-precise far-future timestamp). Note: idempotency_keys has NO updated_at column and NO BEFORE UPDATE trigger -- the peer-row byte-identity probe relies on the per-column projection across all 13 observable columns without a trigger-managed drift anchor (vs BE-0466 dokploy_refs which anchors on the set_updated_at trigger). Note: the response_status BETWEEN 100 AND 599 CHECK is unreachable from the typed Complete API (it pre-rejects out-of-range as apierr.Internal); same for the status IN ('pending', 'completed') CHECK (Claim hard-codes 'pending', Complete hard-codes 'completed'); same for the length() > 0 CHECKs on principal_id/idempotency_key/route/request_hash (Claim pre-validates these as apierr.InvalidInput). These CHECKs are pinned at the DB level only and NOT probed from Go in the invariants file -- the file header MUST document this delegation so a future reader does not mistake the absence for a coverage gap. New helpers introduced (per-file Idempotency-suffix to avoid store_test collisions): rawIdempotencyRow (with NULLABLE *int / *time.Time fields so pending NULLs are distinguishable from zero-value scalars), loadIdempotencyRowByID, countIdempotencyRowsForOrg, mintIdempotencyID, idempotencyTxRollbackSentinel. REUSED verbatim from sibling idempotency_test.go: idemFixture, claimIdem, completeIdem, findIdem, wantErrCode, newIdempotencyStore; from schema_test.go: seedOrg; from store_test.go: newStore. NO migration added. The byte-identical-bystander tenant-isolation gap-fill is the paired BE-0470 story. Forward-applicable to any future PRD CRUD-and-invariants story whose underlying table has a claim-protocol-style upsert on a non-PK UNIQUE tuple with a multi-column completion-consistent CHECK (e.g. a future webhook-delivery-claims or rate-limit-burst-tokens table).
ralph/progress.txt:216:- Repository CRUD-and-invariants tests for a **TABLE WHOSE SCHEMA ALREADY EXISTS FROM AN EARLY MIGRATION BUT WHOSE TYPED REPOSITORY IS THE FIRST STORY-LEVEL CALLER** -- i.e. the table landed in an ancient migration (dokploy_refs in 0002_tenant_hierarchy) and has been exercised only by raw-SQL schema_test.go tests; the CRUD-and-invariants PRD story creates BOTH the *_repository.go file AND the *_repository_invariants_test.go file from scratch (BE-0465 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/dokploy_ref.go + internal/controlplane/store/dokploy_ref_repository_invariants_test.go). Five load-bearing differences from a first-story-for-table CRUD vertical (BE-0457 deployments, BE-0463 job_attempts, etc.): (a) NO migration is added because the table already exists -- grep the schema_test.go assertions BEFORE writing to learn the column shape and the CHECK / UNIQUE / FK invariants the raw-SQL tests already pin; (b) the typed repository MUST NOT add columns the table lacks even if a comment in another migration suggests otherwise -- migration 0011_optimistic_versions's preamble claims to cover dokploy_refs 'for parity' but its actual ALTER statements only touch organizations / projects / environments / services, so the DokployRef struct MUST NOT carry a Version field and the repository MUST NOT include version in dokployRefColumns or scan it (the single fastest way to discover this kind of drift is the error-chain unwrap debug pattern: write a one-shot test that prints %T:%s for each `cur.(unwrapper).Unwrap()` layer, which surfaces the underlying *pgconn.PgError with the SQLSTATE 42703 'column X does not exist' message in one round trip); (c) if the table has NO customer-mutable column the typed repository surface is INSERT + GET + LIST + DELETE only -- no Update method, no optimistic-concurrency If-Match path -- and the file header MUST document this as load-bearing so a future reader does not mistake the absence for a coverage gap (the 'optimistic versioning where applicable' acceptance-criteria phrase narrows to 'not applicable here'); (d) when the table has a bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY (the FIRST and ONLY int64 PK in the store package), the typed struct's ID field is int64 (not string), the INSERT statement OMITS id from the column list entirely (any value supplied -- even NULL or DEFAULT -- trips SQLSTATE 428C9 'cannot insert a non-DEFAULT value into column "id"'), and the typed error payload renders the int64 id via a private package-local helper rather than importing strconv -- keeping the store package's 'no strconv' invariant intact. A test InsertIgnoresCallerSuppliedID is the regression backstop (a caller that smuggles ID: 999_999_999 must still see the database-minted id in the returned struct); (e) when the table has TWO UNIQUE constraints, one GLOBAL across tenants (e.g. (dokploy_resource, dokploy_id) on dokploy_refs -- a Dokploy object cannot be claimed twice across tenants) and one PER-TENANT (e.g. (organization_id, yalla_id, dokploy_resource, dokploy_id)), BOTH must be exercised explicitly by the invariants tests -- the global-UNIQUE test must seed orgA's row then try to insert orgB's row with the SAME (dokploy_resource, dokploy_id) and assert countRowsForOrg(orgB) == 0 after the rejection. The 19-test shape this file lands: Insert-row-shape (raw-SQL byte-equal), Insert-ignores-caller-supplied-id, four typed-Internal rejections (nil-tx + blank-closed-set-args), four CHECK/FK-violation Conflicts (unknown-yalla_kind, unknown-dokploy_resource, blank-yalla_id, blank-dokploy_id, unknown-organization_id), both UNIQUE violations (global + per-tenant), positive-direction 'multiple domains for same service all persist' (pins the per-tenant UNIQUE includes dokploy_id), Insert-rollback, Get-happy + Get-unknown, GetByDokployTarget-happy + unknown + blank-resource-Internal, ListByYallaResource-ordered + empty + blank-kind-Internal, ListByOrganization-ordered + empty, Delete-happy + unknown + nil-tx, organization-delete-cascade, and a raw-SQL set_updated_at trigger pin (no bump_version trigger exists despite migration 0011's preamble). New helpers introduced (per-file DokployRef-suffix to avoid store_test collisions): rawDokployRefRow, loadDokployRefRowByID, countDokployRefRowsForOrg, mintDokployID, runInsertDokployRefOrFail, dokployRefTxRollbackSentinel. Reused verbatim: seedOrg/seedProject/seedEnvironment/seedService (schema_test.go), newStore (store_test.go). The byte-identical-bystander tenant-isolation gap-fill is the paired BE-0466 story. Forward-applicable to any future first-typed-repository story whose underlying table landed in an early migration and has been carried by raw-SQL schema tests since (a future idempotency_keys repository would inherit the same template, though without the bigint identity PK twist).
ralph/progress.txt:219:- Repository CRUD-and-invariants tests for a **TABLE WHOSE *_repository.go AND PRIMARY *_test.go ALREADY EXIST FROM A PRIOR (NON-PRD) SUBSYSTEM STORY** — i.e. the CRUD-and-invariants PRD story is NOT the first story for the table (BE-0461 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/job_repository_invariants_test.go for provisioning_jobs). Structurally distinct from a "first-story-for-table" CRUD-and-invariants vertical (BE-0457 deployments, BE-0455 quota_reservations, etc.) in three load-bearing ways: (a) NO migration is added — the table landed in an earlier migration (0008_provisioning_jobs.up.sql for provisioning_jobs) and is already exercised by worker-subsystem tests; (b) NO existing helpers may be re-introduced — grep for `jobFixture / insertJob / transitionJob` (or the analogous resource fixtures) BEFORE writing and reuse them verbatim; collisions inside `package store_test` are compile errors; (c) the new file is a GAP-FILL pass against the canonical *_repository_invariants_test.go pattern (deployment_repository_invariants_test.go is the closest template), NOT a reimplementation. Mandatory gap-fill checklist (the 7-test shape this file lands): InsertMintsRowShape (byte-equal raw-SQL re-load vs Insert's RETURNING projection across every column, INCLUDING nullable resource-target legs which must round-trip as SQL NULL not empty string), InsertDuplicateIDReturnsConflict (PRIMARY KEY collision with a DIFFERENT idempotency key — distinct from the UNIQUE (organization_id, idempotency_key) violation already pinned), InsertRollbackPersistsNoRow (sentinel-error rollback + countXxxRowsForOrg == 0 after), GetUnknownReturnsNotFound (same-tenant unknown id, NOT the cross-tenant variant — pgx.ErrNoRows must map to typed apierr.NotFound), ListByOrganizationReturnsReverseChronological (explicit sleeps + 3 rows + assert ordered created_at DESC, id DESC), ListByOrganizationEmptyReturnsEmptySlice (pin len == 0 rather than nil-vs-empty because some repos legitimately return either), ListByOrganizationClampsNonPositiveLimit (probe BOTH limit=0 AND limit=-1 — if the implementation used the caller's value as a literal LIMIT, both would return 0 rows). New helpers introduced (unique to this file, with a per-file `Job` suffix to avoid the package-shared `store_test` collisions): rawJobRow, loadJobRowByID, countJobRowsForOrg, mintJobID, jobTxRollbackSentinel. Reused verbatim: jobFixture, insertJob, transitionJob (from job_test.go), seedOrg/seedProject (schema_test.go), newStore (store_test.go). The file header MUST document what it deliberately does NOT re-cover (validation guards / nil-Tx / state-machine paths / lease-claim / cross-tenant reads / closed-set status / redactor / payload marshalling are pinned by job_test.go and job_internal_test.go) so a future reader does not mistake the absence for a coverage gap. Special-case: the provisioning_jobs row body has THREE concurrent CHECK constraints (finished_consistent, lease_consistent, attempts_bounded) but Insert's coercion of Status='queued' / Attempts=0 / no lease columns makes them UNREACHABLE from public Insert input — they are Transition-time invariants, already pinned by job_test.go's Transition* suite; the invariants file does NOT try to drive them via Insert. Forward-applicable to any future "Repository: <existing-table> CRUD and invariants" story whose table already has worker-subsystem tests (e.g. future audit_events / job_attempts / dokploy_refs / idempotency_keys if their repos land before their PRD CRUD stories).
ralph/progress.txt:220:- Repository tenant-isolation tests for an **APPEND-ONLY CHILD table whose ONLY public mutation surface is Append() inside a *Tx, anchored by a composite FK (organization_id, deployment_id) to a tenant-scoped parent table with ON DELETE CASCADE, whose row body has NO version column AND NO updated_at column AND NO Update/Delete surface (BEFORE UPDATE trigger rejects every UPDATE at the SQL level), AND whose tenant teardown is reachable only TRANSITIVELY via parent-deployment-cascade OR parent-organization-cascade** (BE-0460 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/deployment_event_tenant_isolation_test.go). Structurally distinct from BE-0458 (deployments — lifecycle-bearing table with version + updated_at trigger pair and a Cancel state-transition surface) in five load-bearing ways: (a) there is NO bump_version trigger AND NO set_updated_at trigger to anchor a cross-row UPDATE detection, because the BEFORE UPDATE trigger rejects every UPDATE outright; the bystander byte-identity assertion is therefore really proving Append's INSERT statement never even ATTEMPTS a write outside the supplied (organization_id, deployment_id) tuple. (b) The cross-tenant guard test must seed a SYMMETRIC PAIR of forged Append attempts — (orgA, bravoDep) AND (orgB, alphaDep) — and probe deployment_events by the forged ids to assert count = 0 for BOTH; a single direction would miss a regression that derived organization_id from the deployment row at write time, AND a missing forged-id-was-never-persisted probe would miss a buggy retry loop that swallowed the FK error after a phantom INSERT. (c) The cascade variants are TRANSITIVE and must be tested at BOTH depths separately — parent-deployment delete cascade (composite FK ON DELETE CASCADE on deployments(organization_id, id)) AND parent-organization delete cascade (the transitive chain organizations -> deployments -> deployment_events) — because each FK leg's organization_id scoping must be proven independently; a regression that dropped the organization_id leg of either FK would surface as either a bravo row vanishing or its byte content drifting. (d) The "soft-deleted rows where applicable" acceptance-criteria phrase narrows to "where applicable": deployment_events has no soft-delete column and no Delete repository surface, so cascade is the analogous proof and is the substitute test surface — the file header MUST document this delegation explicitly so a future reader does not mistake the absence for a coverage gap. (e) The metadata column is jsonb stored as []byte; bystander byte-identity uses bytes.Equal not json equivalence (a regression that stripped or reordered keys would defeat json equivalence but survive bytes.Equal — and the test wants to catch ANY rewrite, semantic or representational). The 8-test shape (Append-bystander-byte-identity across queued+running+progress peers / Append-row-count-invariant / Append-foreign-parent-deployment-rejected-BOTH-directions-with-forged-id-not-persisted+bystander-byte-identity / GetByID-cross-tenant-NotFound-with-no-leak (message/metadata key/value/request_id/correlation_id/event_type/deployment_id) / ListByDeployment-cross-tenant-empty-and-counts-unchanged across per-org AND per-deployment probes / ListByDeployment-foreign-deployment-id-of-SAME-tenant-returns-empty (composite (organization_id, deployment_id) probe distinct from "foreign org+foreign deployment") / parent-deployment-delete-cascade-is-tenant-scoped across queued+running+progress orgB peers / parent-organization-delete-cascade-is-tenant-scoped) leaves BE-0459's invariants surface (CRUD/append-only-trigger/closed-set-event_type/composite-FK/duplicate-id/nil-tx/rollback/metadata-redaction/caller-supplied-occurred_at/cascade-on-deployment) untouched. Helpers reused from BE-0459: rawDeploymentEventRow, loadDeploymentEventRowByID, countDeploymentEventRowsForDeployment, mintDeploymentEventID, runAppendDeploymentEventOrFail, seedDeploymentEvent; from BE-0458: seedTwoTenantDeploymentFixture, twoTenantDeploymentFixture type; from BE-0457: seedQueuedDeployment; from store_test: seedOrg/seedProject/seedEnvironment/seedService, newStore, containsValue. New helpers introduced (unique to this file): twoTenantDeploymentEventFixture + seedTwoTenantDeploymentEventFixture (the two-tenant boilerplate that ALSO seeds one parent deployment per tenant, because events need a parent deployment as the FK target — a pure two-tenant org/proj/env/svc fixture is insufficient for events), assertDeploymentEventByteIdentical (anchored on every column INCLUDING metadata []byte via bytes.Equal; NO version/updated_at columns to anchor cross-row UPDATE detection), countDeploymentEventRowsForOrg (per-tenant aggregate variant of countDeploymentEventRowsForDeployment — the cascade tests need both, per-tenant is the cascade-scoped probe, per-deployment is the Append-scoped probe). Forward-applicable to any tenant-isolation story whose table is an append-only child anchored by a composite FK to a tenant-scoped parent with cascade (e.g. future audit_events / job_events / log lines stories).
ralph/progress.txt:221:- Repository tenant-isolation tests for a **TENANT-SCOPED LIFECYCLE-BEARING table whose Insert is caller-id, whose state-transition surface is a single Cancel() with optimistic-concurrency disambiguation, whose row body carries a lifecycle CHECK and a bump_version + set_updated_at trigger pair, AND whose global PRIMARY KEY (id) makes deployment ids strictly per-tenant-distinct even though every OTHER per-row anchor (UNIQUE (organization_id, idempotency_key), source_ref, source/status taxonomies) is per-tenant** (BE-0458 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/deployment_tenant_isolation_test.go). Structurally distinct from BE-0456 (quota_reservations — INSERT-only mutation surface, no Cancel, the row body has nullable string columns) in five load-bearing ways: (a) the bystander byte-identity assertion is anchored on TWO trigger-managed columns simultaneously — set_updated_at refreshes updated_at on any matched UPDATE AND the bump_version trigger increments version on the same UPDATE — so a regression that swept a peer tenant's row through a missing organization_id predicate surfaces as either an updated_at drift OR a version bump, not just one. (b) The Insert positive-direction pair is "same-(idempotency_key, source_ref) in two tenants both persist" instead of "same-resource in two tenants both persist", because the only per-tenant UNIQUE on this table is (organization_id, idempotency_key); the test must also assert FindByIdempotencyKey resolves the SAME key to the per-tenant row never cross-tenant — this is the read-side proof of the positive-direction shape. (c) The cross-tenant Cancel probe MUST seed BOTH a target row and an UNRELATED sibling for the same orgA, then byte-identity-assert BOTH after orgB's Cancel — without the sibling, a regression that widened the predicate by status alone (status IN ('queued','running')) would touch every queued row across both tenants and the target-only assertion would miss it. (d) The not-found payload for cross-tenant GetByID must name ONLY the deployment_id the caller supplied — explicit no-leak substring assertions against the foreign source_ref / idempotency_key / service_id pin this; the apierr.NotFound("deployment", deploymentID) call site is the chokepoint. (e) The acceptance criterion "List queries return stable pagination without leaking total counts from other tenants" requires asserting BOTH the empty slice AND that the per-tenant COUNT(*) for the foreign tenant is unchanged after the read — the read must not leak into a write. The 9-test shape (Insert-bystander-byte-identity across queued+running+idem-collide peers / Insert-row-count-invariant / Insert-same-idempotency-key-in-two-tenants-both-persist with cross-FindByIdempotencyKey read-side proof / GetByID-cross-tenant-NotFound-with-no-leak / FindByIdempotencyKey-cross-tenant-returns-zero-no-leak / Cancel-cross-tenant-leaves-target+sibling-byte-identical / ListByService-cross-tenant-empty-and-counts-unchanged / ListByService-foreign-service-id-of-SAME-tenant-returns-empty (composite (organization_id, service_id) probe distinct from "foreign org+foreign svc") / organization-delete-cascade-is-tenant-scoped across queued+running+extra orgB rows) leaves BE-0457's invariants surface (CHECK/FK/duplicate/nil-tx/rollback/lifecycle disambiguation) untouched. Helpers reused from BE-0457: rawDeploymentRow, loadDeploymentRowByID, countDeploymentRowsForOrg, mintDeploymentID, runInsertDeploymentOrFail, seedQueuedDeployment, seedRunningDeployment; from store_test: seedOrg/seedProject/seedEnvironment/seedService, newStore, containsValue. New helpers introduced (unique to this file): twoTenantDeploymentFixture + seedTwoTenantDeploymentFixture (the two-tenant boilerplate is centralised because every test in this file needs both halves), assertDeploymentByteIdentical (anchored on updated_at + version simultaneously), timePtrEqualDep / fmtTimePtrDep (per-file Dep-suffix is mandatory — store_test already exposes QR, QP, and "" variants; collisions block compilation). Note that rawDeploymentRow has NO nullable string columns, so stringPtrEqualDep is deliberately not added (would be U1000 dead code). GOTCHA: when two tenants need to share a row "shape" in the cascade test, the suffixes MUST be per-tenant-unique because mintDeploymentID hashes `t.Name() + suffix` and the GLOBAL PRIMARY KEY on deployments(id) means two tenants cannot share an id even though every OTHER column is per-tenant unique — a naive same-suffix-for-both-tenants call trips mapWriteError's generic "a deployment with this idempotency key already exists" message which LOOKS like an idem-key conflict but is actually an id collision. Forward-applicable to any tenant-isolation story whose table carries a global PRIMARY KEY AND a per-tenant UNIQUE on a different column AND lifecycle triggers (e.g. future BE-0461 provisioning_jobs).
ralph/progress.txt:222:- Repository CRUD-and-invariants tests for a **TENANT-SCOPED LIFECYCLE-BEARING table whose Insert is caller-id (no per-row id minting in the repository), whose only public state-transition surface is a single Cancel() with optimistic-concurrency disambiguation, and whose row body carries a lifecycle CHECK (terminal-status <-> finished_at) plus a bump_version trigger** (BE-0457 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/deployment_repository_invariants_test.go). Structurally distinct from BE-0455 (quota_reservations — the repository mints qres_<token> ids and has no Update/Cancel surface) in three load-bearing ways: (a) the test must mint deployment ids itself (DeploymentService.Create owns domain.NewID(domain.KindDeployment); the repo does NOT mint). A test-local mintDeploymentID(t, suffix) helper using `"dep_"+t.Name()+"_"+suffix` avoids global PK collisions across parallel cases without a shared atomic counter — every *_test.go under internal/controlplane/store/ shares one package, so t.Name() is the natural disambiguator. (b) The terminal-status-on-Insert probe leverages the deployments_finished_consistent CHECK: Insert's INSERT column list omits finished_at, so any terminal Status passed in violates the CHECK by construction (status terminal AND finished_at NULL). This is the one-line proof that this surface can only mint non-terminal rows; no separate trigger test is needed. (c) Cancel exercises THREE disambiguation legs against the SAME *Tx — nil expectedVersion + terminal row -> apierr.Conflict (no current_version attached), non-nil expectedVersion + version mismatch -> apierr.ConflictStale (current_version attached, verified via apierr.CurrentVersionOf), unknown id -> apierr.NotFound. The cross-tenant Cancel probe MUST include a byte-identity assertion on the foreign-tenant row (status unchanged, finished_at still NULL, version not bumped) — without it, a regression that scoped the UPDATE by id alone and disambiguated by organization_id would silently mutate the foreign tenant's row before failing the lookup. The 31-test shape (Insert: row-shape / status-default / non-queued-status-persists / dup-id / dup-(org,idem) / blank-id / blank-requested_by / blank-idem / unknown-source / terminal-without-finished / unknown-org / cross-tenant-service / nil-tx / rollback; GetByID: persisted-row / unknown / cross-tenant; FindByIdempotencyKey: present / missing / cross-tenant; Cancel: queued / running / matching-version / stale-version-ConflictStale / terminal-row-Conflict / unknown-NotFound / cross-tenant-NotFound-with-byte-identity / nil-tx; ListByService: reverse-chronological / empty-non-nil-slice / cross-tenant-empty) leaves the BE-0458 tenant-isolation file to add the bystander byte-identical proof + organization-delete-cascade probes. Helpers reused: seedOrg/seedProject/seedEnvironment/seedService (schema_test.go), newStore (store_test.go). New helpers introduced (unique to this file): rawDeploymentRow, loadDeploymentRowByID, countDeploymentRowsForOrg, mintDeploymentID, runInsertDeploymentOrFail, seedQueuedDeployment, seedRunningDeployment, deploymentTxRollbackSentinel.
ralph/progress.txt:223:- Repository tenant-isolation tests for a **TENANT-SCOPED EVENT-LIKE LEDGER table whose ONLY public mutation surface is an unbounded INSERT (no UNIQUE on (organization_id, resource) — only PRIMARY KEY (id) and a composite-FK target UNIQUE (organization_id, id)), whose ONLY read aggregate is a SUM filtered by tenant + resource + status + expires_at predicates, and whose row body carries nullable timestamp/text columns** (BE-0456 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/quota_reservation_tenant_isolation_test.go). Structurally distinct from BE-0454 (quota_usage — partial UNIQUE (organization_id, resource) and the ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING / SELECT FOR UPDATE primitive) in three load-bearing ways: (a) the bystander byte-identity assertion must cover the nullable job_id and settled_at columns (rawQuotaReservationRow declares them *string/*time.Time; the per-file stringPtrEqualQR/timePtrEqualQR/fmtStringPtrQR/fmtTimePtrQR helpers MUST get a per-file QR suffix because store_test already exposes QP-suffixed variants from BE-0451 and a stringPtrEqual variant from BE-0440); (b) the same-resource-in-two-tenants positive-direction pair is INSERT-and-both-persist (no conflict at all — no UNIQUE constraint to defeat) instead of LockUsage-and-both-succeed; (c) a NEW cross-tenant probe is mandatory — global PRIMARY KEY collision across tenants must surface as typed apierr.Conflict (yerr.CodeConflict) AND leave the prior owner's row byte-identical AND insert no row for the requester; this guards against a regression that swapped the INSERT for an ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE (a copy-paste of quota_policies' upsert pattern). The 7-test shape (insert-bystander-byte-identity across active+settled peers / insert-row-count-invariant / same-resource-two-tenants-both-persist / global-PK-collision-cross-tenant-conflict with byte-identical prior-owner / SumActiveReservations-tenant-scoped (10-vs-300 chosen so a cross-tenant fold shows as 310) / SumActiveReservations-on-empty-tenant-returns-zero-even-when-others-have-rows / organization-delete-cascade-is-tenant-scoped) leaves the BE-0455 invariants tests (status / time / resource predicate filters, CHECK / DOMAIN / FK violations, row-shape, id-provenance, rollback, nil-tx) untouched. Helpers reused: seedOrg, insertReservationRow, insertSettledReservationRow, loadQuotaReservationRowByID, countQuotaReservationRowsForOrg, runInsertReservationOrFail, newStore. New helpers introduced (unique to this file): assertQuotaReservationByteIdentical (anchored on updated_at via the BEFORE UPDATE quota_reservations_set_updated_at trigger).
ralph/progress.txt:224:- Repository CRUD invariants test file for a **TENANT-SCOPED COUNTER table whose ONLY public mutation surface is an INSERT...ON CONFLICT (organization_id, resource) DO NOTHING + SELECT...FOR UPDATE primitive (LockUsage), with NO per-row Update/Delete, NO version column, NO service_id pointer, and a database-managed used_value mutated by an EXTERNAL code path (quota.Checker, not the repository), set_updated_at trigger fires BEFORE UPDATE only** (BE-0453 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/quota_usage_repository_invariants_test.go). Structurally distinct from BE-0451 (quota_policies — INSERT...ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE upsert) in four load-bearing ways: (a) the second-call byte-identity proof must anchor updated_at TOO — ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING is not an UPDATE and the set_updated_at trigger must not fire (a regression that switched to ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE with even a no-op SET would silently bump updated_at and a missing anchor would let that slip past); (b) the peer-row proof is a SAME-TENANT-DIFFERENT-RESOURCE check (UNIQUE (organization_id, resource) isolates rows by resource within a tenant); (c) the row-already-exists-with-non-zero-value leg has no policies analog — pre-seed via raw SQL at a sentinel value (e.g. used_value=17, id=`qu_seed_<resource>`) and prove BOTH the seeded id AND the seeded used_value survive a LockUsage call (this is the ONLY way to prove the discarded INSERT's minted prefix id does not replace the row's id — a regression to ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE SET id=EXCLUDED.id would be a catastrophic data-loss path that typed reader projections would not catch); (d) the DOMAIN-violation test casts an arbitrary string to the typed Resource (e.g. `store.QuotaResource("not-a-resource")`) to bypass Go's compile-time check and exercise the DOMAIN quota_resource CHECK at the database layer — surfacing as yerr.CodeConflict through mapWriteError. The 8-new-test shape (row-shape, second-call-byte-identity, peer-row-byte-identity, persisted-used-value, unknown-org-Conflict, unknown-resource-Conflict, tx-rollback, nil-tx-Internal) leaves the pre-existing TestQuotaRepositoryLockUsageCreatesAndReturnsCounter in quota_repository_test.go alone as the load-bearing two-call-idempotency probe. Helper symbols introduced: `lockUsageOrFail`, `loadQuotaUsageRow`, `countQuotaUsageRowsForOrg`, `rawQuotaUsageRow`, `quotaUsageTxRollbackSentinel`. Reused symbols from sibling files: `seedOrg` (schema_test.go), `seedQuotaUsage` (usage_test.go), `newStore` (store_test.go) — note `seedQuotaUsage` is the ONLY way to set used_value to a non-zero value through a test fixture because the repository surface has no equivalent (LockUsage cannot increment).
ralph/progress.txt:227:- Repository CRUD invariants for a **multi-table resource FAMILY whose physical realization is one table per scope in the Organization -> Project -> Environment -> Service hierarchy, sharing identical CHECK / FK / CASCADE / bump_version / set_updated_at invariants but with per-scope mutation surfaces** (BE-0449 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/{organization,project,environment,service}_variable_repository_invariants_test.go). A PRD note that reads "Database table: <name>" can refer to such a family (variables = 4 scope tables, quota_* = 3 ledger tables) — when the family is hierarchical, ship one *_repository_invariants_test.go per physical table rather than collapsing into a single mega-file, because per-scope mutation surfaces diverge (organization_variables uniquely exposes UpdateMutable + DeleteByKey on top of the bulk-replace surface; project / environment / service variables expose only the bulk Upsert + DeleteByXxxExceptKeys pair). Each file in the family extends the BE-0447 grant-table invariants template with three uniform substitutions: (a) the Upsert payload struct is the scope's XxxVariableUpsert (Value, IsSecret, SecretProvider, SecretKeyID, SecretCiphertext) instead of role + principal_kind; (b) a brand-new secret_columns_consistent CHECK-violation test is added (IsSecret=true with non-empty Value triggers the secret branch's `value=''` clause and surfaces as typed apierr.Conflict — exhaustively probing the OTHER drift directions is needless because the repository's nullableSecretColumns helper makes them structurally impossible from the caller side); (c) the leaf-scope variants (env / service) add a cross-tenant composite-FK test mirroring BE-0447's service-grant cross-tenant proof. Every *_test.go file under internal/controlplane/store/ shares the same store_test package, so the per-scope helpers MUST get distinct names — newOrganizationVariableID / newEnvironmentVariableID / newServiceVariableID, upsertOrganizationVariable / upsertEnvironmentVariable / upsertServiceVariable, listOrganizationVariablesOrFail / listEnvironmentVariablesOrFail / listServiceVariablesOrFail, and organizationVariableTxRollbackSentinel / projectVariableTxRollbackSentinel / environmentVariableTxRollbackSentinel / serviceVariableTxRollbackSentinel. The project-variables file is the smallest because project_variable_test.go already covers Upsert INSERT/UPDATE happy paths, cross-tenant rejection, the bulk delete surface, and the nil-Tx guards — its invariants file pins ONLY the row-shape and lifecycle invariants the existing tests do not anchor (version=1 default, byte-equal created_at == updated_at, ON CONFLICT id+created_at preservation, PK reuse -> Conflict, secret CHECK violation, ON DELETE CASCADE, Upsert tx-rollback). Forward-applicable to any future multi-table family at this hierarchy (a hypothetical KindOrganizationSetting / KindProjectSetting / KindEnvironmentSetting / KindServiceSetting feature would inherit this 4-file template structure).
ralph/progress.txt:228:- Repository CRUD invariants test file for a **tenant-scoped LEAF-grant table whose ONLY mutation surface is an INSERT...ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE upsert PLUS a bulk DELETE-by-exclusion, with a database-managed version column, NO nullable scope pointer below the parent FK, and a composite FK to a leaf-entity parent (organization_id, service_id) -> services** (BE-0447 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/service_grant_repository_invariants_test.go). Structural reduction of the BE-0443 environment_grant_repository_invariants_test.go template: every leg that exercised a nullable *string scope pointer collapses or drops out — the round-trip pointer test is replaced by a service-account-principal-kind test (the closed-set CHECK leg still needs proving), the NULLS-FIRST ordering assertion in ListByService is gone (two-column ORDER BY principal_id, id), the empty-string-COALESCE dedup equivalence leg is gone (no COALESCE in the unique index), and the upsert/replace input struct drops the trailing *string field. The Upsert signature is six-positional `(id, organization_id, parent_id, principal_id, principal_kind, role)`; the ON CONFLICT branch keys on the three-tuple `(organization_id, parent_id, principal_id)`. The principal-kind cross-smuggling invariant MUST be preserved verbatim (re-upsert with a different caller-supplied principal_kind for the same principal_id; assert persisted kind = baseline). The duplicate-PK test must use a DIFFERENT principal_id so the ON CONFLICT branch does NOT match and the PRIMARY KEY (id) uniqueness violation is exercised instead of the upsert path. The ON DELETE CASCADE proof must delete the parent via raw SQL (the ServiceRepository.ScheduleDeletion is a SOFT delete and would NOT trigger CASCADE). The Upsert rollback proof reduces to ListByService-only because no per-id Get exists; ListByService is the only observation surface for a grant row. NEW Kind constant in domain/id.go MUST be added to BOTH the const block AND the canonical `kinds` map at the bottom — Valid() and the ID parser read from the map, not the const list, so a missing map entry silently breaks NewID(KindFoo) with ErrUnknownKind. For brand-new tables, services already exposes UNIQUE (organization_id, id) from migration 0002 so NO companion ALTER TABLE is needed on the parent (UNLIKE BE-0435 api_keys parent). Forward-applicable to any future grant table whose parent IS the leaf of the Organization -> Project -> Environment -> Service hierarchy or whose parent is otherwise structurally leaf-like (a hypothetical KindDeploymentGrant / KindJobGrant would inherit this template).
ralph/progress.txt:229:- Repository CRUD invariants test file for a **tenant-scoped child table whose ONLY mutation surface is an INSERT...ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE upsert PLUS a bulk DELETE-by-exclusion (no per-id Get, no per-id Delete, no traditional UPDATE), with a database-managed version column, optional nullable scoping pointers, and a composite FK to a parent (organization_id, project_id) -> projects** (BE-0439 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/project_grant_repository_invariants_test.go). Two structural divergences from BE-0435 (api_key_scope_repository_invariants_test.go) are load-bearing: (1) the Upsert ON CONFLICT branch must be tested with a DIFFERENT caller-supplied id AND a DIFFERENT caller-supplied principal_kind to prove BOTH the caller-id-is-ignored guarantee AND the cross-kind-smuggling-impossible guarantee — the principal_kind column is NOT part of the scope tuple, so an existing row stays at its current principal_kind even when the caller passes a different kind for the same principal_id; a single Role-only update test would silently pass while a regression that promoted principal_kind to a SET clause still went undetected. (2) The Upsert rollback proof reduces to ListByProject-only because the repository exposes no per-id Get for grants — ListByProject is the ONLY observation surface for a grant row, so it is the load-bearing leak channel a partial commit would surface through, and an absent Get assertion is a deliberate file boundary, not a gap. Insert duplicate-id test must use a DIFFERENT principal_id so the ON CONFLICT (org, proj, principal, env, svc) branch does NOT match and the PRIMARY KEY (id) uniqueness violation is exercised — otherwise the upsert path silently swallows the duplicate id. DeleteByProjectExceptIDs has TWO codepaths in the repository (len(keepIDs)==0 -> unconditional DELETE; len>0 -> DELETE WHERE id NOT IN (ANY $3::text[])), so BOTH must be exercised independently — the empty-keep test must additionally use a fresh seed after the nil-keep clear AND assert the empty-slice ([]string{}) form is identical to nil at the wire (the repository documents nil and empty as identical, so a regression that diverged them would surface here). ON DELETE CASCADE from projects is the project-grant analogue of api_key_scope's ON DELETE CASCADE from api_keys: use raw `DELETE FROM projects WHERE id=$1` (NOT the repository's ScheduleDeletion, which is a SOFT delete and would NOT trigger CASCADE) and observe via ListByProject. Helper `newProjectGrantID(f, label)` synthesises a `pgrnt_<token_n>` id by rewriting f.Project's `prj_<token_n>` prefix — the testutil.Factory only stamps known domain prefixes and there is no f.ProjectGrant, so prefix-rewriting is the cleanest way to mint factory-unique ids without reaching into domain.NewID(domain.KindProjectGrant). The sibling project_grant_test.go's `seedProjectGrant` raw-SQL helper is kept intentionally separate: that file's tests exercise the read path against rows the schema (not the repository) inserted, so a regression that broke INSERT would still let the read tests pass — by contrast THIS file's `upsertProjectGrant` exercises the repository's INSERT path, so the two seed surfaces are complementary, not duplicates.
ralph/progress.txt:230:- Repository CRUD invariants test file for a **tenant-scoped table whose mutation surface spans FOUR distinct UPDATE paths (Update / UpdateDisplayName / ScheduleDeletion / Restore) PLUS Insert, with a database-managed version column AND a nullable soft-delete column (deletion_scheduled_at)** (BE-0437 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/project_repository_invariants_test.go): this is the BE-0425 organization template extended with the second-UPDATE-path projection (UpdateDisplayName must run the dual-anchor AND prove the path-specific WHERE/SET isolation — the row's slug must be untouched since the SET clause is `SET display_name = $3` only) and the BE-0431 inverse-soft-delete projection (Restore must dual-anchor AND clear deletion_scheduled_at back to NULL — the trigger fires on any UPDATE so a forgotten `SET deletion_scheduled_at = NULL` clause would still pass the version/timestamp anchors, making the "deletion_scheduled_at is nil after Restore" assertion the load-bearing one). Insert rollback proof must span ALL read surfaces the rolled-back row touches: by-id Get, by-org ListByOrganization, AND CountByOrganization (the count the quota layer reads), so a future refactor that switched Insert to a SAVEPOINT or autonomous transaction would leak the row into a quota check even though Get reports NotFound. The nil-Tx typed-Internal guard only needs to close the ONE mutation path the existing tests did not cover (here UpdateDisplayName) — duplicating Insert/Update/ScheduleDeletion/Restore guards already proved elsewhere is noise. Test fixtures compose seedOrg + projectFixture(f.Project(org, label)) + insertProject helpers from project_test.go; do not re-implement them at package scope.
ralph/progress.txt:231:- Repository CRUD invariants test file for a **tenant-scoped grandchild table whose mutation surface is Insert + Update + ScheduleDeletion ONLY (NO UpdateDisplayName, NO Restore), with a database-managed version column, a CHECK-constrained closed-set Kind column, a nullable soft-delete column (deletion_scheduled_at), and a composite FK to a parent (organization_id, project_id) -> projects** (BE-0441 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/environment_repository_invariants_test.go): the BE-0437 project_repository_invariants_test.go template MINUS the UpdateDisplayName and Restore tests (the EnvironmentRepository exposes neither method — adding empty stubs that reference store.EnvironmentRepository.UpdateDisplayName / Restore would fail to compile), so the invariants matrix shrinks from six DB-backed tests to four (Insert dual-fields-anchor, Update dual-anchor, Update matching-If-Match positive, ScheduleDeletion dual-anchor) PLUS the Insert-rollback and Insert-nil-Tx pair. Update's preserved-identity assertion gains a Kind-preservation check (the SET clause is `SET slug = $3, display_name = $4` only, so kind is structurally untouched even though the column exists on this table) that the projects template did not need (projects has no kind column); ScheduleDeletion's preserved-identity assertion ALSO gains a Kind check for the same reason. The nil-Tx typed-Internal matrix-closer is Insert (NOT UpdateDisplayName as in projects) because environment_update_test.go and environment_delete_test.go already cover Update and ScheduleDeletion respectively — the matrix-closer convention is "fill the missing path", and the missing path is table-specific. New file-scope helpers `environmentFixture` and `insertEnvironment` mirror projectFixture/insertProject but environmentFixture MUST default Kind to store.EnvironmentKindStandard (the migration 0025 CHECK constraint requires "standard" or "preview" — empty string fails) — the raw-SQL seedEnvironment in schema_test.go relies on the database column default, but the repository's INSERT path supplies kind explicitly. Rollback proof scope is by-id GetByID + parent-scoped ListByProject (there is no CountByOrganization equivalent on environments — quota counts environments per-project, not per-org, and per-project counts are not yet exposed on the repository surface; do NOT extend the rollback assertion to a non-existent method). Rollback sentinel type is environmentTxRollbackSentinel — every *_repository_invariants_test.go in store_test contributes its own uniquely-named sentinel.
ralph/progress.txt:234:- Repository tenant-isolation test file for a **tenant-scoped grandchild table whose ONLY read surface is a parent-scoped ListByProject (no per-id Get, no per-id Delete), with TWO mutation paths (Upsert with ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE PLUS DeleteByProjectExceptIDs whose len(keepIDs)==0 and len>0 codepaths must each be exercised), a database-managed version column, optional nullable scoping pointers (environment_id, service_id), and a composite FK to a parent (organization_id, project_id) -> projects** (BE-0440 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/project_grant_tenant_isolation_test.go). This is the BE-0436 api_key_scope_tenant_isolation template adapted for a grandchild table whose conflict target itself includes (organization_id, project_id) AND a multi-column scope tuple. Five projections api_key_scopes had no analogue for are load-bearing here: (a) the byte-identical-bystander proof is observed through ListByProject (the only read surface) — there is no per-id Get like api_key_scopes had, so a helper getGrantFromListOrFail must scan the list response for the bystander id and fatal on absence; the implementation favours correctness over efficiency because the bystander lists are 1-2 rows by construction. (b) the byte-identical-bystander assertGrantByteIdentical helper must compare TWO nullable *string pointer columns (environment_id, service_id) in addition to the scalar columns — pair-helper stringPtrEqual / fmtStringPtr emit "<nil>" vs quoted string for failing assertions so nullability and value drift surface side-by-side; api_key_scopes had no pointer columns, so this is the first tenant-isolation file with a *string comparison. (c) the load-bearing worst-case fixture is the shared-scope-tuple fixture (both tenants own a grant row with the SAME (principal_id, environment_id, service_id) tuple on their OWN projects) AND the Upsert UPDATE-branch bystander test re-upserts with a DIFFERENT role + a fresh caller-supplied id to BOTH fire the ON CONFLICT branch on orgA AND prove the caller-id is ignored — a regression that resolved the conflict by scope-tuple alone (without organization_id and project_id in the conflict target) would have UPDATEd orgB on the peer-trigger fire. (d) DeleteByProjectExceptIDs needs TWO bystander tests because the repository has two SQL codepaths (one unconditional DELETE for nil/empty keepIDs, one DELETE WHERE id NOT IN ANY for non-empty keepIDs); the len>0 test must seed orgB grants whose id is NOT in keepIDs so a regression that dropped the tenant predicate would have deleted them through the "NOT IN ANY" branch. (e) ListByProject cross-tenant projectID returns an empty non-nil slice, so the cross-tenant indistinguishability projection is len==0 + non-nil + unknown-projectID-under-own-tenant yields the SAME empty-non-nil shape (NOT the Code+Hint NotFound shape from api_key_scopes / projects, because grants are read through a list path, not a per-id Get). Helpers seedOrg / seedProject / newProjectGrantID / upsertProjectGrant / listGrantsOrFail are reused from project_grant_test.go and project_grant_repository_invariants_test.go in the same store_test package — sibling-file test name collisions (TestProjectGrantRepo* in project_grant_test.go, TestProjectGrantRepository* in project_grant_repository_invariants_test.go) MUST be checked with `grep '^func Test'` on BOTH sibling files before adding new test names; this file uses the TestProjectGrantRepository* prefix (matching the invariants file convention) with unique suffixes (...OnOrgADoesNotTouchOrgB, ...IsolatesSharedPrincipal, ...InTwoTenantsBothSucceed). The file's docstring MUST call out which legs are already proved elsewhere (cross-tenant FK Conflict, duplicate-PK Conflict, ON DELETE CASCADE, tx-rollback, nil-Tx guards in BE-0439; ListByProject ordering and basic cross-tenant empty-list property in project_grant_test.go) and which are NEW HERE (byte-identical bystander on version + updated_at + role + pointer columns across BOTH Upsert branches AND BOTH DeleteByProjectExceptIDs codepaths, indistinguishability of cross-tenant vs unknown projectID at the list path, per-tenant scope-tuple coexistence, shared-principal_id read isolation) so a future reader does not look for missing tests where the proof is intentionally elsewhere.
ralph/progress.txt:242:- Repository CRUD invariants test file for a **composite-key tenant-scoped link table** with a database-managed version column (BE-0429 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/membership_repository_invariants_test.go). The BE-0427 user_repository_invariants_test.go shape is the closest template, but four substitutions are mandatory because the row has no own-id, has a (organization_id, user_id) composite primary key, and carries role_version + the memberships_set_updated_at trigger: (a) the duplicate-Insert Conflict test asserts on the PK collision (a second Insert with the same (org, user) pair) instead of the citext-UNIQUE-email collision — the schema has no UNIQUE column outside the PK that an INSERT could violate; (b) the existing membership_test.go's UpdateRole case already asserts the role_version atomic +1 increment, so the invariants file's UpdateRole test is dedicated to the timestamp dual-anchor (updated_at refreshed by the BEFORE-UPDATE trigger, created_at preserved) — do NOT duplicate the role_version increment here; (c) NO Delete-cascades-to-children test (the membership row is the SINK of two ON-DELETE-CASCADE FKs from users and organizations, not a SOURCE — cascades INTO memberships are proved in the parent table's invariants file, e.g. TestUserRepositoryDeleteCascadesToMemberships in user_repository_invariants_test.go); (d) the local rollback sentinel type MUST use a distinct name from user_repository_invariants_test.go's `errSentinel` because every *_test.go file under internal/controlplane/store/ shares the same store_test package — `membershipTxRollbackSentinel struct{}` is the canonical naming choice. The Insert-returns-row-with-timestamps test additionally asserts (a) role_version == 1 (the schema default that backs session-token revocation in BE-0020 auth), (b) Email + UserDisplayName populated from the JOINed users row (Insert returns OrganizationMember, not raw Membership — the httpapi layer renders the joined shape directly), and (c) created_at.Equal(updated_at) on the fresh row (the trigger has not fired yet). Seed pattern: seedOrg + seedUserRow first, then call repo.Insert through the repository (NOT seedMembership, which is the raw-INSERT helper for tenant-isolation tests). Forward-applicable to BE-0431/0432 (service_accounts CRUD + tenant-isolation) and BE-0433/0434 (api_keys CRUD + tenant-isolation) — both are tenant-scoped tables with their own version-tracking columns, so the rule "split the version-increment assertion (canonical CRUD test) from the timestamp-dual-anchor assertion (invariants test)" should also apply.
ralph/progress.txt:243:- Repository CRUD invariants test file for a **tenant-scoped table whose only customer mutation is an idempotent lifecycle flip** (BE-0431 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/serviceaccount_repository_invariants_test.go). The BE-0427 user_repository_invariants_test.go and BE-0429 membership_repository_invariants_test.go shapes are the templates, but three substitutions are mandatory because service_accounts has no generic Update and no version column — only Insert + Disable (COALESCE-idempotent): (a) the trigger-dual-anchor test drives through the **lifecycle mutation** (Disable) instead of a generic Update — created_at preserved, updated_at moves forward via the BEFORE UPDATE trigger, plus the lifecycle field (disabled_at) is set and IsDisabled() flips true; (b) the idempotent-second-call semantics are NOT re-asserted here (they belong in the canonical CRUD test in serviceaccount_test.go); (c) the rollback-leaves-zero-footprint test asserts BOTH Get-is-NotFound AND CountByOrganization == 0 (the quota reader is the second observable surface a half-written row could surface through, and a future refactor that switched Insert to a SAVEPOINT or autonomous transaction would silently inflate it without ever surfacing on Get). Distinct sentinel type name (`serviceAccountTxRollbackSentinel`) to avoid the store_test package-level collision with `errSentinel` (user) and `membershipTxRollbackSentinel` (membership). Forward-applicable to BE-0433/0434 (api_keys CRUD + tenant-isolation): api_keys has an Update path (rotate / revoke / patch metadata), so the invariants file should re-introduce the full Update-trigger dual-anchor test from the user/membership template alongside the rollback + nil-Tx guards; the secret-bearing nature of api_keys means the rollback test must additionally assert that no secret_hash needle leaks into post-rollback observable state.
ralph/progress.txt:245:- Repository tenant-isolation test file for a **tenant-scoped row with its own id, organization_id COLUMN, NO version column, NO generic Update, and NO soft-delete column** — only Insert + Disable lifecycle flip (BE-0432 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/serviceaccount_tenant_isolation_test.go). The BE-0428 user_tenant_isolation_test.go and BE-0430 membership_tenant_isolation_test.go shapes are the templates, but four substitutions are mandatory because service_accounts has its OWN id (no shared-identity column the way memberships shares user_id with the global users table) and the load-bearing cross-row failure mode is "two tenants happen to share a slug (UNIQUE (organization_id, slug) permits this by design) and a WHERE-on-slug-only or WHERE-on-id-only mutation swaps the WRONG tenant's row": (a) every byte-identical-snapshot test seeds BOTH tenants with service accounts holding the SAME slug via a `seedServiceAccountWithSlug(t, db, f, org, label, slug)` helper that overrides the factory's slug — a WHERE-on-slug mistake would resolve to whichever row the planner found first and the per-tenant id assertion would catch it (the BE-0430 "same user, distinct role/role_version tuples" framing translates here to "different ids, SAME slug"; the inverse is the only cross-row leak surface a tenant-root-with-its-own-id table exposes); (b) the Disable bystander test is the analogue of BE-0430's UpdateRole bystander — Disable is the only mutation beyond Insert, so the BE-0428 generic Update test does not apply; the trigger dual-anchor (created_at preserved + updated_at refreshed) PLUS the lifecycle field (disabled_at == nil) on the bystander row catch any WHERE-on-slug-only Disable that ever matched the wrong row; (c) the duplicate-Insert Conflict bystander test snapshots BOTH the conflicting target tenant's row AND the same-slug-different-tenant bystander row AND a post-state CountByOrganization == 1 for each (since the trigger is BEFORE-UPDATE only, a rolled-back INSERT that resolved its UNIQUE check against the cross-tenant row would have no trigger anchor — Count is the second observable surface a row landing in the wrong tenant could leak through, mirroring the BE-0431 invariants-file Count-after-rollback rule); (d) the cross-tenant-NotFound-vs-unknown-id indistinguishability test must run for BOTH Get AND Disable (the only two NotFound-emitting paths the repository exposes) — divergence in Code or Hint is an existence leak surface, and the mutation path is a separate code path from the read; afterward, re-read the foreign tenant's row and assert disabled_at == nil so the cross-tenant Disable probe did not silently flip the lifecycle. The List isolation test takes TWO shapes that must both run (mirrors BE-0430): (i) disjoint-fixture count isolation (orgA=1 SA, orgB=3 SAs, gotA must contain only saA's id, gotB must contain exactly {saB1, saB2, saB3} — proves no JOIN scrambled which rows land where); (ii) shared-slug-row isolation (saA and saB both with slug "shared-ci-slug", List(orgA) returns exactly one row with id=saA, List(orgB) returns exactly one row with id=saB — proves a SELECT disambiguating by slug instead of by organization would have either swapped or merged the two rows). One additional CountByOrganization-specific test seeds three tenants (orgA=2, orgB=3, orgC=0 empty bystander) and asserts the three distinct counts; this is the quota-layer leak surface (Count is the read the quota engine consults to enforce per-organization limits, so a leak inflates one tenant's effective quota with another's usage). Document the deliberate ABSENCE of soft-delete tests (service_accounts has no deletion_scheduled_at — only disabled_at, which the byte-identical Disable snapshot treats as the analogue) AND the deliberate ABSENCE of generic-Update / DELETE / cascade tests (Disable is the only mutation; the api_keys.service_account_id cross-tenant FK leak proof lives in serviceaccount_test.go's TestServiceAccountAPIKeyCannotCrossTenantBoundary; the cascade-on-org-delete proof lives in TestServiceAccountBelongsToOneOrganization and TestTenantHierarchyCascadeDelete) in the file's package-doc comment so a future reader does not look for a missing test (mirrors BE-0428/BE-0430's deliberate-absence note). Note on apierr.NotFound message vs Hint: NotFound("service_account", id) Newf-builds a Message containing the caller-supplied id verbatim, but never sets Hint — so the cross-tenant-vs-unknown-id indistinguishability check MUST compare Code and Hint only (Message would diverge by caller input, which is not a leak). Forward-applicable to BE-0434 (api_keys tenant-isolation: same shape with the same "two tenants share a slug-equivalent" angle — api_keys has UNIQUE (organization_id, prefix), so the shared-prefix fixture is the structural counterpart; plus a secret-bearing extension — the cross-tenant probes must additionally assert no secret_hash needle reaches any observable surface) and BE-0436 (api_key_scopes tenant-isolation).
ralph/progress.txt:246:- Repository CRUD invariants test file for a **tenant-scoped row with its own id, organization_id COLUMN, NO version column, THREE distinct UPDATE paths (UpdateMutable / Revoke / RotateCredential), an additional UPDATE-but-not-mutation-state path (TouchLastUsed), and a secret-bearing row body (secret_hash + globally-UNIQUE prefix)** (BE-0433 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/api_key_repository_invariants_test.go). The BE-0427 user_repository_invariants_test.go, BE-0429 membership_repository_invariants_test.go, and BE-0431 serviceaccount_repository_invariants_test.go shapes are the templates, but five substitutions are mandatory because api_keys has no version column, three UPDATE paths instead of one, a separate touch-style UPDATE for the auth path, and a secret-bearing row body: (a) the trigger-dual-anchor (created_at preserved + updated_at refreshed by the BEFORE UPDATE trigger) must run ONCE PER UPDATE PATH — UpdateMutable, Revoke, RotateCredential, and TouchLastUsed each get their own dedicated dual-anchor test, so a refactor that dropped the trigger from one path but not the others trips a path-specific gate (the BE-0431 single-Disable pattern is too weak here); (b) the UpdateMutable dual-anchor test additionally pins the **mutable surface** to exactly {name, scopes} by snapshotting every non-mutable column (prefix, secret_hash, created_by, service_account_id, expires_at, revoked_at, last_used_at) as byte-identical to the baseline — UpdateMutable is the first repository method in the store package whose SET-clause is a true subset of the table's mutable columns, so the non-mutation assertion is the only structural defense against a future refactor that quietly widened the SET-clause; (c) RotateCredential gets a dedicated test that pins the **credential swap** invariant: prefix and secret_hash flip to the new values, every identity/ownership/scopes/lifecycle column stays byte-identical, the OLD prefix no longer FindByPrefix-resolves (must surface yerr.CodeNotFound — rotation is the only mutation that orphans a previous lookup key), and the NEW prefix re-binds to the SAME key id (rotation is a credential swap, not a re-mint, so audit trails and outstanding access grants keep pointing at the same id); (d) the rollback-leaves-zero-footprint test asserts the standard Get-NotFound + ListByOrganization-empty, AND adds two **secret-bearing redaction probes** specific to api_keys: SELECT COUNT(*) FROM api_keys WHERE secret_hash = $1 must return 0 AND SELECT COUNT(*) FROM api_keys WHERE prefix = $1 must return 0 — a SAVEPOINT-or-autonomous-transaction refactor that survived the outer rollback would surface here even when every per-id read reports NotFound, because the credential primitives (the secret hash an attacker would need, and the public prefix that drives the authentication lookup) are the load-bearing observables the table owes its caller; (e) typed-Internal nil-Tx guards run for ALL FIVE mutation paths (Insert / UpdateMutable / Revoke / RotateCredential / TouchLastUsed) — the pre-existing TestAPIKeyRepositoryInsertRejectsNilTx in apikey_test.go only asserts err != nil (weaker than the typed-Internal contract every other repository follows), so the typed-code test is additive, not a replacement. Distinct sentinel type name (`apiKeyTxRollbackSentinel`) to avoid the store_test package-level collision with `errSentinel` (user), `membershipTxRollbackSentinel` (membership), and `serviceAccountTxRollbackSentinel` (service account). Local `equalTimePtr(a, b *time.Time) bool` helper is the canonical way to express "lifecycle stamp untouched" concisely without dragging in a third-party deep-equality helper — both-nil OR both-non-nil-and-time.Equal. Forward-applicable to BE-0434 (api_keys tenant isolation: the cross-tenant probes must additionally assert no secret_hash needle reaches any observable surface — same secret-bearing redaction probe pattern documented above for the rollback test, but applied to the cross-tenant-Get / cross-tenant-FindByPrefix / cross-tenant-List read paths and to the typed error surfaces Code/Hint/Message) and to ANY future repository whose row body carries a secret-bearing column (api_key_scopes does NOT — the scope-name column is non-secret — but variables.value when is_secret=true IS, so the BE-0451+ variables invariants stories should reuse this secret-bearing-rollback-probe rule).
ralph/progress.txt:247:- Repository tenant-isolation test file for a **tenant-scoped row whose mutation surface spans FIVE distinct methods (UpdateMutable / Revoke / RotateCredential / TouchLastUsed plus the Insert path) AND whose row body carries TWO load-bearing credential primitives (a globally-UNIQUE prefix and a secret-bearing secret_hash)** (BE-0434 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/api_key_tenant_isolation_test.go; the cross-tenant analogue of BE-0433 and the secret-bearing variant of the BE-0432 service_accounts shape). Two schema-level facts make this pattern structurally distinct from BE-0432: (i) api_keys.prefix is GLOBALLY UNIQUE (NOT UNIQUE (organization_id, prefix) — the prefix authentication lookup runs BEFORE the caller's tenant is known) so the BE-0432 "two tenants share a slug" worst-case fixture has no row-level analogue here; the cross-tenant prefix-conflict path is still load-bearing (orgA Insert with orgB's already-persisted prefix AND orgA RotateCredential to orgB's prefix) and BOTH paths must surface CodeConflict, leave orgB's bystander row byte-identical, and leave NO row anywhere carrying the rolled-back secret_hash needle (raw-SQL probe). (ii) api_keys carries a secret-bearing column — secret_hash — so the cross-tenant byte-identical-bystander assertion includes a `assertRawSecretHashUnchanged` raw-SQL probe AGAINST the typed-read SecretHash field (the typed read path is already covered by the struct snapshot, but a regression that scanned a non-secret column into the struct or skipped the field on Get would silently pass the struct check while the underlying row drifted — the raw probe is the load-bearing safety net for THAT class of regression specifically), and the cross-tenant indistinguishability test additionally asserts the secret_hash needle does NOT appear in any of the typed-error surfaces yeCross.Error() / yeCross.Message / yeCross.Hint across ALL five mutation methods (the secret-bearing redaction probe extension to the BE-0432 Code+Hint indistinguishability rule). Skeleton: one t.Parallel test per (mutation × leg) plus a 5-row indistinguishability-and-redaction table test that iterates (Get, UpdateMutable, Revoke, RotateCredential, TouchLastUsed). Three load-bearing rules: (1) Every byte-identical-bystander test asserts BOTH the trigger-managed updated_at (the api_keys_set_updated_at BEFORE-UPDATE trigger refreshes it on every matched UPDATE, including a WHERE-on-id-only UPDATE that touched the wrong tenant) AND the lifecycle column the OWNING method writes (revoked_at for Revoke, last_used_at for TouchLastUsed, prefix+secret_hash for RotateCredential, name+scopes for UpdateMutable) so a WHERE-on-id-only regression surfaces independently on EITHER anchor. (2) The Insert cross-tenant prefix-conflict test must add a SELECT COUNT(*) FROM api_keys global-table-size probe AFTER the conflicting Insert — a rolled-back Insert that landed its row in some OTHER tenant's slot would show up there even if the per-id probe missed it; the per-id probe alone is insufficient because the failing row's id is not known to the probe. (3) FindByPrefix is INTENTIONALLY NOT a cross-tenant probe surface — the signature carries no caller-tenant parameter, the prefix column is globally unique, and the resolved row carries its own organization_id; document this absence in the test file's package-doc comment so a future reader does not look for a missing test (mirrors BE-0426's "tenant-root tables have no ListByParent" note). Helper shape worth pinning forward: a `getXOrFail(ctx, t, s, repo, orgID, id, label) X` and a `writeX(ctx, s, fn) (X, error)` pair keep byte-identical snapshot tests free of Read/Write closure boilerplate; combined with a single `assertBystanderByteIdentical(t, label, baseline, after)` helper they reduce a 12-column equality check to two lines per (mutation × leg). Forward-applicable to BE-0436 (api_key_scopes tenant isolation — a child-table variant with NO secret-bearing column and only one mutation surface, so the secret-bearing redaction probe and most of the conflict-rollback shape collapse; the LIST-by-parent isolation rule remains) and to BE-0450 (variables tenant isolation, where the secret-bearing column is value WHEN is_secret=true, so the rolled-back-secret probe rule applies CONDITIONALLY on is_secret — the same shape extended with a per-row classification check). Use docker compose up -d postgres + YALLA_TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgres://yalla:yalla@127.0.0.1:5432/yalla go test -race -count=1 -run 'TestAPIKeyRepository.*OrgADoesNotTouchOrgB|TestAPIKeyRepositoryGetReturnsCorrectRowAcrossTenants|TestAPIKeyRepositoryInsertCrossTenantPrefixConflictDoesNotTouchOrgB|TestAPIKeyRepositoryRotateCredentialCrossTenantPrefixConflictDoesNotTouchOrgB|TestAPIKeyRepositoryListByOrganizationCountsAreIsolated|TestAPIKeyRepositoryListByServiceAccountIsTenantScoped|TestAPIKeyRepositoryCrossTenantErrorIsIndistinguishableFromUnknownId' ./internal/controlplane/store/ to verify; the suite skips cleanly when the env var is unset. Same pre-existing -race-with-DB unrelated regressions noted under BE-0426/BE-0432 still apply — confirmed by stashing the new file and re-running, the failing tests are environment-service / api-key-service Create paths, organization_variable_patch audit metadata, and TestSQLArgsAreConstantExpressions; none are touched by this pattern.
ralph/progress.txt:355:- Cross-cutting capability package + at-rest encryption seam (BE-0339 pattern, internal/controlplane/secrets/{doc.go, provider.go, aesgcm.go, plaintext.go, *_test.go, AGENTS.md} + internal/controlplane/store/migrate/migrations/0029_organization_variables_encrypted_secrets.{up,down}.sql + internal/controlplane/store/organization_variable.go + organization_variable_service.go + organization_variable_secrets_at_rest_test.go + cmd/yalla-api/main.go buildSecretsProvider; structural template for BE-0340..0342 sibling at-rest stories (project / environment / service variable encryption-at-rest) and any future cross-cutting source-of-truth seam over a same-shape resource family — webhook signing secrets, deployment-config secrets, backup-target credentials, etc.). Six load-bearing rules for the cross-cutting package: (1) Land the package ONCE under internal/controlplane/<pkg>, with the Provider interface + a production implementation (AESGCM) + a tests-only passthrough implementation (Plaintext, restricted to local/test profiles by cmd/yalla-api) + a package AGENTS.md that documents BOTH the runtime contract AND the forward-applicable storage pattern (column shape, CHECK constraint shape, repository tuple shape, service-layer seam location). (2) Typed value-free errors (ErrInvalidCiphertext / ErrUnknownKey / ErrUnsupportedProvider) — the secrets package contract never echoes any of {plaintext, ciphertext bytes, key material, key id, nonce} into an error message; the only observable on a failure is the classification. (3) AES-256-GCM with per-Seal random crypto/rand nonce + AEAD-authenticated decryption + multi-key rotation pool (first key is active for Seal, all keys accepted for Open). KeyID derivation = first 8 bytes of SHA-256(key) hex-encoded (16 chars): deterministic across processes, non-secret (one-way hash), short enough for audit metadata. NewAESGCM rejects duplicate keys at construction so a misconfiguration that silently reduces the rotation pool is caught fast. (4) cmd/yalla-api wires the provider in ONE buildSecretsProvider helper near runStartupChecks, NOT one per scope. Strict profiles (staging/production) refuse to start without YALLA_SECRET_KEYS (config.requireStrictFields enforces) via an explicit sentinel error; permissive profiles (local/test) fall back to Plaintext with a Warn log line. (5) The interface is INTENTIONALLY same-width every scope needs (org/project/env/service variables), so a single provider configured at startup covers every variables table — sibling at-rest stories become mechanical clones of schema + service + test changes. (6) For internal-only read paths that legitimately need the plaintext (the future Dokploy renderer worker, BE-0343 variable-inheritance resolver), Open the sealed tuple through the same Provider passed into the service — NEVER a wire-facing endpoint. Six load-bearing rules for the storage shape per table: (1) Add three nullable columns (secret_provider text NULL, secret_key_id text NULL, secret_ciphertext bytea NULL) ALONGSIDE the existing plain `value` column, never instead of it; force value='' for secret rows and cipher columns NULL for non-secret rows via ONE CHECK constraint named <table>_secret_columns_consistent that pins the invariant in both directions. The CHECK is the defence-in-depth backstop the service relies on — a bug that tries to write a plaintext secret rolls back as deterministic apierr.Conflict rather than silent corruption. (2) Mirror DO $$ guards in BOTH the up and the down migration that REFUSE to run while is_secret=true rows exist with the wrong shape — a re-application against a database that accumulated plaintext secrets fails loudly rather than silently leaving them behind, and a rollback against a database with sealed rows fails loudly rather than silently losing every secret value. (3) Add a partial index on (secret_provider, secret_key_id) WHERE is_secret=true so the future key-rotation worker (BE-0351) can locate rows referencing a retired key without a full-table scan. (4) The repository's Upsert + UpdateMutable signatures take a closed-set Input tuple (<Table>VariableUpsert with Value/IsSecret/SecretProvider/SecretKeyID/SecretCiphertext) — keeps the signature narrow as new secret fields land and centralises the "nullable columns when is_secret=false" projection in one helper (nullableSecretColumns). (5) The OrganizationVariable struct (and siblings) gain the three encryption-at-rest fields as INTERNAL-only — never on the wire — and the LogValue redaction MUST be updated in the SAME edit to redact BOTH the plaintext AND the new ciphertext to output.Sentinel; an unstale slog record that captures the struct cannot leak either. (6) scanRow projection routes through sql.NullString for the nullable text columns (pgx-scan into *string rejects NULL). Five load-bearing rules for the service-layer seam: (1) Seal at the SERVICE layer, never the repository — Seal is a side-effecting call that depends on a runtime Provider not a *Tx, calling Seal BEFORE opening the transaction shortens the SQL critical section, and the repository stays pure SQL. The cleanest helper is a one-line projector (sealVariableValue) that takes the customer's input shape (<Table>VariableReplace) and returns the repository's tuple shape (<Table>VariableUpsert) — non-secret items pass through untouched, secret items go through Provider.Seal. (2) The Service's constructor (NewOrganizationVariableService) takes Provider as a REQUIRED dependency rejected at construction if nil — a production process cannot accidentally start with the at-rest seam disabled. (3) Patch path for sealed rows has THREE post-state cases the service must enumerate explicitly: (a) caller supplied a new value → use it verbatim; (b) caller supplied only is_secret AND the current row is_secret → Open the current ciphertext via Provider.Open to recover the plaintext, then re-Seal under the active key (the only correct way to re-key a secret without surfacing the plaintext to the caller); (c) otherwise → use the current row's `value` column. Skipping case (b) would either lose the secret bytes on a re-seal or write a "previously-sealed plaintext" string into the value column. (4) The demotion path (is_secret=true→false) reuses case (b) to recover the plaintext, then writes it back to the value column with NULL encryption columns — the ONLY customer path that surfaces a previously-sealed plaintext, and only as a deliberate operator action. (5) Seal failures surface as apierr.Internal with a value-free wrapped cause — a sealing error is a server-side problem, never customer input, and the wrap never echoes the plaintext. Six load-bearing rules for the integration-test surface: (1) The integration test file lives next to the existing repository tests as <table>_secrets_at_rest_test.go, uses testutil.RequireMigratedDB (skips cleanly when YALLA_TEST_DATABASE_URL is unset so go test ./... stays green without Postgres), and wires a REAL AES-256-GCM provider (mustAESGCM helper that mints a fresh 32-byte key per test so parallel tests cannot bleed key ids across each other). (2) Five pinned tests per scope: SealsSecretValuesAtRest (the load-bearing contract — secret value reaches Postgres ONLY as AES-GCM ciphertext under the active key id, value column is "", on-disk bytea does not contain the plaintext substring, ciphertext round-trips through Provider.Open, audit row never carries any of {plaintext, key name, hex ciphertext, key id, provider id}); PatchRotatesCiphertextOnEveryWrite (a patch that flips is_secret only on a sealed row Opens-then-Seals under a fresh nonce so on-disk bytes change between writes); PatchDemotesSecretToPlaintext (is_secret=true→false with no new value recovers the plaintext via Open and writes to the value column with NULL encryption columns); SchemaRejectsPlaintextSecretWrite (three direct INSERT attempts that would land a plaintext-secret row each surface a constraint violation whose error message names the constraint); LogValueRedactsCiphertextAndPlaintext (a structured log record that captures the variable struct redacts BOTH plaintext AND ciphertext to output.Sentinel while exposing provider id + key id). (3) For the existing tests, fixture-shape changes (seedOrganizationVariable wrote plaintext into the value column for is_secret=true rows) need updating IN PLACE rather than introducing a second helper — the new helper writes the encryption columns under the Plaintext provider's wire identifiers ("plaintext-v1" / "plaintext") for is_secret=true fixtures, so every legacy seedOrganizationVariable(..., true) call site keeps working. (4) Pair the fixture change with a package-level test helper (revealVariableValue) that projects OrganizationVariable.Value back to the plaintext for both shapes — for the Plaintext provider, ciphertext IS the plaintext bytes, so the helper is one branch (if IsSecret → string(SecretCiphertext) else v.Value). Tests that previously asserted v.Value == "expected" become revealVariableValue(v) == "expected" with no further restructuring. (5) ActorKind in audit-fed test inputs MUST be one of {"", "usr", "sa", "system"} — the audit_events.actor_kind CHECK constraint will reject "user" / "human" / any other value as a 23xxx that surfaces as a misleading "an audit event with this id already exists" via mapWriteError's canonical conflict message. The actual diagnostic is the actor_kind CHECK, not duplicate id. (6) When extracting Value/Plaintext assertions out of failing tests against a contract change, FIRST stash the working tree and re-run the failing tests on HEAD to separate "I introduced this failure" from "this was already failing"; the diff of failure name sets is the cleanest signal that the new code is at parity with the baseline. BE-0339 used this to confirm 23 of the 25 store-wide failures and 3 of the 12 org-variable failures were pre-existing. NOT applicable to BE-0343 (variable inheritance resolver) — that story consumes the at-rest seam through Provider.Open against multiple scopes simultaneously and needs its own pattern around the merge order + per-scope Open call site. NOT applicable to BE-0351 (secret encryption key rotation) — that story consumes the partial index organization_variables_secret_routing_idx + the KeyIDs() / ActiveKeyID() surface to enumerate rows referencing a retired key and re-Seal them, but the rotation worker is a separate operational concern from the at-rest write path this pattern owns.
ralph/progress.txt:373:- POST sub-action endpoint on a deep resource by bare id (BE-0163 pattern, POST /v1/environments/{environment_id}/clone, store/environmentservice.go EnvironmentService.Clone + CloneEnvironmentInput + validateCloneEnvironmentInput, httpapi/environments.go cloneEnvironmentHandler + EnvironmentCloner): canonical shape for ALL sub-action POSTs on a bare-id route (clone, restart, rollback, cancel, restore, etc.). The path carries the SOURCE id and a verb suffix, and the resolver pins only (OrganizationID, ID) — scoped grants pinning ProjectID are denied by covers() (one-way containment, the same boundary GET/PATCH/DELETE inherit on the bare-id route family). The structural parent_id of the new row (project_id for env.clone, etc.) is DERIVED FROM THE SOURCE ROW inside the same tx, NEVER from the body or path — the request body strictly omits the parent_id field, and the strict decoder rejects any caller who tries to supply one (proven by an explicit RejectsUnknownField contract test). The service-layer orchestrator composes Create-style steps PLUS a source-row read at the front PLUS a lifecycle-state guard before any write: read source under (orgID, sourceID) → reject DeletionScheduledAt != nil as Conflict (a lifecycle-dead row is not a valid source) → in-tx Authorize → Quota.Reserve → Repository.Insert (the new row inherits source.ProjectID) → JobEnqueue → Audit.Append with Metadata{"slug","project_id","cloned_from": source.ID} (the cloned_from / restored_from / rolled_back_to entry records non-secret structural provenance — the audit trail's only handle on "where did this row come from"). Reuse the EXISTING action constant rather than minting a sub-action one (environment.create on a clone route, not environment.clone): the PRD-mandated action is the same CapWrite, same role matrix, same grant containment, and a new action constant would force a sweep of the action catalog, builtinRoleCaps, every policy matrix test, and the OpenAPI surface for zero contract benefit. The HTTP handler reuses environmentIDResolver (the bare-id family's shared resolver), returns 201 Created (a Create-style sub-action), and mirrors row.Version into the ETag header via writeOrganizationETag. Service-level tests MUST seed with seedDomainOrg / seedDomainProject / seedDomainEnvironment helpers (canonical 26-char-suffix IDs via domain.MustNewID — the testutil factory's IDs are deliberately non-canonical and the service's strict ParseID validation correctly rejects them); repository-level tests can use either helper. NewHandler+newRouteTable each grow ONE positional port (environmentCloner EnvironmentCloner at the tail of the env-ports group, after environmentDeleter); the three-pattern sweep across ~38 test files anchors on `fakeEnvironmentDeleter{}, nil)` → `fakeEnvironmentDeleter{}, fakeEnvironmentCloner{}, nil)`, plus the `fakeEnvironmentDeleter{})` newRouteTable form → `fakeEnvironmentDeleter{}, fakeEnvironmentCloner{})`, plus the all-nils OpenAPI-shape tests bumped from N to N+1 nils. cmd/yalla-api/main.go threads environmentService as the cloner — the single concrete *EnvironmentService satisfies Creator/Reader/Updater/Deleter/Cloner together.
ralph/progress.txt:562:- Persistence access goes through internal/controlplane/store: Store.Read (read-only tx -> Querier) and Store.Write (read/write tx -> *Tx; commit on nil, rollback on ANY error or panic). A *Tx is obtainable ONLY inside Store.Write; repository mutation methods take *Tx, read methods take Querier — so a mutation structurally cannot run outside the transaction that also carries its authorization and quota checks. A unit of work validates input first, then composes authorize -> reserve quota -> write desired state -> enqueue durable job inside one Store.Write. The policy/quota/jobs dependencies are narrow port interfaces defined IN store (Authorizer, QuotaReserver, JobEnqueuer); store never imports those packages. Error mapping is uniform: pgx.ErrNoRows -> apierr.NotFound, SQLSTATE class 23 -> apierr.Conflict, other driver errors -> apierr.StoreUnavailable; the raw cause is wrapped for logging only. ProjectRepository/ProjectService are the reference implementation.
ralph/progress.txt:807:- Repository CRUD invariants test file for a **tenant-scoped child table whose ONLY mutation surface is an INSERT...ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE upsert PLUS a bulk DELETE-by-exclusion (no per-id Get, no per-id Delete, no traditional UPDATE), with a database-managed version column, ONE optional nullable scoping pointer (service_id) instead of two (no environment_id pointer because the env IS the parent), and a composite FK to a parent (organization_id, environment_id) -> environments** (BE-0443 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/environment_grant_repository_invariants_test.go). This is the BE-0439 project_grants template adapted for environment_grants. Three structural divergences from BE-0439 are load-bearing: (1) the EnvironmentGrant struct has NO ProjectID column and ONE optional pointer (ServiceID) instead of two (EnvironmentID is the mandatory FK target, not an optional scope leg) — the Upsert insert-pointer-roundtrip test collapses from two opt-in pointers to one (service-scoped grant). (2) The ON CONFLICT target is the unique index environment_grants_principal_scope_idx on (organization_id, environment_id, principal_id, COALESCE(service_id, '')) — there is no environment_id leg in the COALESCE because the environment is the parent scope, not an optional narrower; the duplicate-PK Conflict test uses a different principal_id under the same environment to dodge the scope-tuple branch and exercise the PRIMARY KEY (id) violation. (3) The CASCADE-delete-of-parent test deletes the environments row directly via raw SQL (NOT the EnvironmentRepository's ScheduleDeletion, which is a soft delete and would NOT trigger CASCADE) and observes via ListByEnvironment. Helper newEnvironmentGrantID synthesises an `egrnt_<token_n>` id by rewriting f.Environment's `env_<token_n>` prefix — the testutil.Factory only stamps known domain prefixes and there is no f.EnvironmentGrant, so prefix-rewriting is the cleanest way to mint factory-unique ids without reaching into domain.NewID(domain.KindEnvironmentGrant). Sentinel type `environmentGrantTxRollbackSentinel` is distinct from every other *_test.go file's rollback sentinel in store_test. List-helper name `listEnvironmentGrantsOrFail` is distinct from project_grant_repository_invariants_test.go's `listGrantsOrFail` to avoid same-package collision. Test name prefix `TestEnvironmentGrantRepository*` (full Repository) is distinct from environment_grant_test.go's `TestEnvironmentGrantRepo*` and `TestEnvironmentGrantReader*` siblings — no collisions.
