ralph/progress.txt:19811:The 20-test shape pins: Append-mints-id-and-row-shape (depev_ prefix), Append-blank-fields-default (Message='' RequestID='' CorrelationID='' Metadata=nil), Append-respects-caller-supplied-id, Append-respects-caller-supplied-occurred-at (worker-reconciliation contract), Append-unknown-event-type-rejected (CHECK via mapWriteError -> Conflict), Append-blank-event-type-rejected (apierr.Internal at application boundary, NOT a database-CHECK pass-through — the empty string is a programming error caught before SQL, since a blank value would also pass the CHECK if the CHECK accepted '' which it does not but the application boundary catches it first), Append-unknown-deployment-rejected (composite FK -> Conflict), Append-cross-tenant-deployment-rejected (composite FK -> Conflict + countDeploymentEventRowsForDeployment proves no row persisted), Append-duplicate-id-Conflict (PRIMARY KEY collision via mapWriteError), Append-nil-Tx-Internal, Append-rolls-back-on-tx-rollback, Append-peer-row-byte-identity (NO updated_at column to drift, so the test asserts byte-identity on id+EventType+Message+OccurredAt+CreatedAt — the absence of bump_version + set_updated_at triggers MEANS the peer can never drift on a successful sibling Append), Update-rejected-by-trigger (raw `db.Exec UPDATE` bypasses the repository surface entirely so the trigger is the only thing that can stop the write), cascade-delete-on-deployment (raw `DELETE FROM deployments WHERE id = $1` cascades through the composite FK ON DELETE CASCADE), GetByID-returns-persisted-row / unknown-NotFound / cross-tenant-NotFound-with-no-leak-on-foreign-deployment-id, ListByDeployment-chronological-order (seed three rows OUT OF chronological order so the ASC ordering must reorder them on read), ListByDeployment-unknown-deployment-empty (returns empty slice not error — distinguishes "no events" from "no parent" is the caller's responsibility, mirrors deployment ListByService), ListByDeployment-cross-tenant-empty. The peer-row test gets simpler than quota_reservations or deployments because there is NO updated_at column to drift and NO version column to bump — append-only is structural in the column list, not just the trigger. Forward-applicable to every future append-only event-log surface (e.g. provisioning_job_events when that lands).
ralph/progress.txt:20141:  - Bare-{id} CapDeploy verb engine pins (job.retry BE-0279, future job.cancel BE-0282, future deployment-by-id mutate verbs like deployment.cancel-by-id / deployment.rollback-by-id at /v1/deployments/{deployment_id}/cancel / .../rollback) follow the EXACT SAME containment shape as the bare-{id} CapRead pin (job_read_byid BE-0276) with three mechanical differences. (1) The role matrix flips from six-row allow (CapRead) to four-row allow / two-row deny (CapDeploy) — Viewer and Support deny ReasonDeniedNoCapability in the role matrix and in the org-grant capability matrix. (2) The cross-tenant matrix flips Support from allow (ReasonAllowedBySupport via the CapRead+CapSupport exception) to deny (ReasonDeniedCrossTenant — CapDeploy is OUTSIDE the exception). (3) Project/env/service grant fixtures use RoleAdmin (or RoleCI for the explicit CI pin) instead of RoleViewer because Viewer doesn't hold CapDeploy and would deny on capability rather than scope — RoleAdmin keeps the test ANCHORED on scope containment, with one explicit "viewer on target project denies no capability" subtest in the project-grant suite to anchor on capability instead. Pattern locks in for BE-0282 (job.cancel) which is the IDENTICAL CapDeploy + bare-{id} story for the cancel verb — mechanical clone of this file by s/Retry/Cancel/ + s/retry/cancel/ + s/ActionJobRetry/ActionJobCancel/.
ralph/progress.txt:20144:  - The destructive-verb framing for CapDeploy job verbs differs from the CapDeploy preview verbs (preview.delete BE-0270) in semantics, not structure: job.retry's "destructive side-effect" is re-issuing a mutating Dokploy provisioning call against the row's owning resource (replaying a desired-state change), NOT removing a Yalla-owned row. The docstring framing therefore emphasises "re-issue mutating Dokploy work", "replay a destructive desired-state change", "unrecoverable side-effect at the upstream provisioner that no in-Yalla rollback can undo" — distinct from preview.delete's "tear down customer-data row, data-loss incident, unrecoverable without backup". Substitution rule: replace data-loss-incident framing with side-effect-at-provisioner framing for any future job.cancel / job.retry / deployment.cancel-by-id / deployment.rollback-by-id pin. The destructive-side-effect framing is now a reusable substitution candidate for any future CapDeploy bare-{id} verb where the side-effect happens at the Dokploy provisioner, not at the Yalla store.
